.TH FATHOM 1  "fathom 0.1.0-alpha.1" 
.SH NAME
fathom \- Review an agent\-driven repository before it merges.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfathom\fR [\fB\-\-plain\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-alt\-screen\fR] [\fB\-\-fresh\fR] [\fB\-\-mouse\fR] [\fB\-\-lines\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\fR] [\fB\-\-demo\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR] [\fB\-\-detectors\fR] [\fB\-\-enable\-detector\fR] [\fB\-\-disable\-detector\fR] [\fB\-\-events\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Review an agent\-driven repository before it merges.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-plain\fR
Draw as text, with no terminal taken: for pipes, CI logs and screen readers (ADR\-006 §6.7).

Chosen automatically when stdout is not a terminal, so a redirected run degrades by itself.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-alt\-screen\fR
Draw in place instead of taking the alternate screen, which keeps the shell\*(Aqs scrollback
.TP
\fB\-\-fresh\fR
Ignore the session this repository has stored, and start from defaults (`e1.11`).

The escape hatch for a session that came back wrong: a filter that hides the episode you are looking for, a cursor on a row that moved. The stored state is not destroyed — a run that changes nothing writes nothing — it is simply not applied to this one.
.TP
\fB\-\-mouse\fR
Take the mouse: click selects, double\-click drills, the wheel scrolls (`e5.5`, ADR\-006 §10.1).

Off by default, because capturing the pointer takes the terminal\*(Aqs own text selection away for the length of the run. Every gesture it adds is also a key, and the status line says `mouse on` while it is armed.
.TP
\fB\-\-lines\fR
With `\-\-plain`: print the frame in reading order, one block per pane, instead of side by side.

Two panes drawn next to each other is the right shape for eyes and the wrong shape for a log, a pipe or a screen reader (ADR\-006 §6.7). Same facts, same order, one per line.
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\fR \fI<NAME|PATH>\fR
Paint the frame with this theme: a built\-in name, a theme file, or a path to one (`e5.6`).

`dark`, `light` and `ansi` are built in, and all three are measured — for contrast against the background they paint, and for how far the added / removed / violation pairs stay apart when protanopia and deuteranopia are simulated. Anything else is `<name>.toml` in `$FATHOM_THEME_DIR` or `~/.config/fathom/themes/`, or a path. `$FATHOM_THEME` and `[theme] name` in your own config are the same knob one layer down; a repository\*(Aqs `fathom.toml` is deliberately not a layer at all, because a clone does not get to repaint your terminal. With colour off — `NO_COLOR`, or a terminal that has none — the theme is not applied and every state keeps its shape and its word. `fathom tui \-\-list\-themes` lists what Fathom can see; `\-\-show\-theme` prints the one in force with its measurements, and `\-\-show\-theme \-\-audit` exits 7 if a palette fails a floor.
.TP
\fB\-\-demo\fR
Review a miniature repository that ships inside this binary, then say what to do next (`e5.9`).

The five\-minute tour: Fathom unpacks a 21\-commit repository into its cache directory, indexes it with the same `fathom index` you would run on your own history, and opens on it. Nothing in the directory you ran it from is read or written, no configuration is read, and deleting `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/fathom` is a complete undo. With a verb the verb runs against the miniature; with none you get the shell.
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR
Print the index\*(Aqs progress row even where stderr is not a terminal (`e5.9`).

On a terminal the row is on by default, one row overwriting itself; this asks for it in a log, a CI step or a `watch \-n 1` wrapper, where one line per update is the point. It applies to `fathom index` and to the indexing `fathom \-\-demo` does on your behalf — no other verb has a row to print — and `\-\-json` refuses it, because that stream belongs to the run report and a row beside a machine\-readable document is how a parser dies.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Never print the index\*(Aqs progress row, even on a terminal (`e5.9`).

The other half of the same choice: a script with its own progress line, or a run whose output is being tee\*(Aqd somewhere, turns it off explicitly instead of discovering it on.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR
Do not watch the repository from an open frame: show what the index holds, and say so (`reco\-bpc`).

An open shell watches its repository by default, so a commit made in another terminal arrives by itself. This turns that off, and the frame then says `snapshot` and what would move it rather than letting a still rail be read as a quiet repository. The other way to get the same picture, cheaper, is to run `fathom watch` in a second terminal and let it own the indexing.
.TP
\fB\-\-detectors\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Which detectors run, moved off the file\*(Aqs answer (`e6.7`).

A comma\-separated spec: `id` or `+id` runs one as well, `\-id` turns one off even if it ships on, `all` bases the set on every built\-in detector, and `none` bases it on nothing. So `\-\-detectors none,+churn_loop` is one detector and `\-\-detectors all,\-secret_string` is eleven. It is the same grammar as `detectors.enabled` and `detectors.disable` in `fathom.toml`, applied after it, and it names a plugin detector by its namespaced id (`\-\-detectors +acme.untested_agent_logic`). A `\-\-detectors` line is what a CI job uses to ask one narrow question without editing a file.
.TP
\fB\-\-enable\-detector\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Run one more detector, by id — the long form of `\-\-detectors +id` (`e6.7`)
.TP
\fB\-\-disable\-detector\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Do not run one detector, by id — the long form of `\-\-detectors \-id` (`e6.7`).

This is the switch for a built\-in you have decided about: the row stays in the report as `disabled`, so "did it look?" still has an answer, and `detectors.suppress` stays the switch for a finding whose family you have muted rather than a detector you have turned off.
.TP
\fB\-\-events\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write this run\*(Aqs analysis event stream to a file, or to stderr with `\-` (`e6.7`).

One JSON object per line, versioned, appended: what started, what each detector did, what every finding said, and how the run ended. `[events] enabled = true` turns the stream on for a repository; this turns it on for one run, over whatever the file says. `fathom events` reads it back.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
fathom\-index(1)
Index the repository so every other verb has something to read
.TP
fathom\-show(1)
Show one episode in full
.TP
fathom\-episodes(1)
List the episodes — the rail\*(Aqs rows, headless (`reco\-11b`)
.TP
fathom\-attribution(1)
Report or correct who Fathom thinks wrote a commit (`e4.1`)
.TP
fathom\-review(1)
The review ledger: sign\-offs that outlive the history they were written about (`e4.6`)
.TP
fathom\-analyze(1)
Print one episode\*(Aqs delta document — the model the TUI, the export and an agent all read
.TP
fathom\-narrative(1)
Read one episode: the narrative every claim in it cites its evidence from (`e4.7`)
.TP
fathom\-drift(1)
What the plan said against what the session did — the contradictions, both sides linked (`e4.8`)
.TP
fathom\-why(1)
Print one episode\*(Aqs risk score with every feature, its weight and its contribution (`e4.4`)
.TP
fathom\-stats(1)
Report repo scale, index state and cache hit rate
.TP
fathom\-check(1)
Run the checks CI cares about; findings above the threshold exit 7
.TP
fathom\-doctor(1)
Describe this build, its environment and its index, with a remedy per finding
.TP
fathom\-export(1)
Write a review artefact for a pull request or an audit
.TP
fathom\-mcp(1)
Speak MCP to a coding agent
.TP
fathom\-watch(1)
Watch the repository and index as it changes
.TP
fathom\-ambient(1)
The glance pane for the terminal beside the agent: latest episode, queue, index, one reason (`reco\-8bx`)
.TP
fathom\-hooks(1)
Report on the harness event stream: lag, offset, rejections, replay (`e6.1`)
.TP
fathom\-daemon(1)
The one process per repository that watches, tails the harness stream and indexes (`e6.3`)
.TP
fathom\-status(1)
Who is keeping this index current, what it costs, and what is left (`e6.3`)
.TP
fathom\-tui(1)
Print the terminal UI\*(Aqs keymap, or open the UI itself
.TP
fathom\-open(1)
Open an episode\*(Aqs file at its line in `$EDITOR` — jump out, do not read around it (`e5.8`)
.TP
fathom\-difftool(1)
Show one file\*(Aqs two sides in your own difftool — difftastic, meld, kdiff3, `vim \-d` (`e5.8`)
.TP
fathom\-cmp(1)
Compare any two episodes or revisions: the composed delta over a range (`e5.3`)
.TP
fathom\-search(1)
Search the whole index from one box, in the shell\*(Aqs own ranking (`e5.2`)
.TP
fathom\-architecture(1)
The architecture scorecard: five dimensions, one number, and the drift since your last review (`e3.9`)
.TP
fathom\-ratchet(1)
The ratchet: the committed allowance ledger, which tightens on its own and widens only when told to (`e3.10`)
.TP
fathom\-context(1)
What this repository knows about a path or an episode: the recorded decisions and the open questions (`e6.5`)
.TP
fathom\-note(1)
The review memory: notes, decisions, questions and follow\-ups, committed under `.fathom/reviews/` (`e6.5`)
.TP
fathom\-completions(1)
Print the shell\*(Aqs own copy of this grammar: completion scripts and man pages (`e7.4`)
.TP
fathom\-events(1)
Read the analysis event stream a run wrote: what happened, in order (`e6.7`)
